WEBVTT

00:01.720 --> 00:03.600
[siren wailing]

00:12.880 --> 00:15.600
["The Mercy Seat" playing]

00:27.040 --> 00:30.320
♪ I began to warm and chill ♪

00:31.440 --> 00:34.640
♪ To objects and their fields ♪

00:34.840 --> 00:38.040
♪ A ragged cup, a twisted mop ♪

00:38.880 --> 00:41.600
♪ The face of Jesus in my soup ♪

00:43.040 --> 00:46.600
♪ Those sinister dinner deals ♪

00:47.360 --> 00:50.680
♪ The meal trolley's wicked wheels ♪

00:51.080 --> 00:54.600
♪ And the mercy seat is waiting ♪

00:54.920 --> 00:58.640
♪ And I think my head is burning ♪

00:58.720 --> 01:00.880
♪ And in a way I'm yearning ♪

01:00.960 --> 01:06.080
♪ To be done with all this
Weighing up of truth ♪

01:06.440 --> 01:10.000
♪ An eye for an eye
And a tooth for a tooth ♪

01:10.240 --> 01:13.720
♪ And anyway I told the truth ♪

01:14.040 --> 01:17.360
♪ And I'm not afraid to die ♪

01:17.480 --> 01:21.040
♪ And the mercy seat is burning ♪

01:21.120 --> 01:24.760
♪ And I think my head is glowing ♪

01:24.840 --> 01:27.120
♪ And in a way I'm hoping ♪

01:27.320 --> 01:32.040
♪ To be done with all this
Weighing up of truth ♪

01:32.440 --> 01:36.240
♪ An eye for an eye
And a tooth for a tooth ♪

01:36.600 --> 01:39.920
♪ And anyway I told the truth ♪

01:40.360 --> 01:45.360
♪ And I'm afraid I told a lie ♪

01:48.040 --> 01:51.280
[Polly screaming] Please,
can we get some help here? Please!

01:51.440 --> 01:53.560
It's all right, Michael.
It's all right, Michael, I'm here.

01:54.440 --> 01:55.800
It's all right, Michael.
You're going to be okay.

01:55.880 --> 01:58.440
Don't leave us now, just keep
prepared around, keep breathing.

01:58.720 --> 02:00.160
[indistinct chatter]

02:01.800 --> 02:06.440
No, no, I don't want fucking
kids in here, I want soldiers.

02:06.520 --> 02:08.960
I don't want fucking kids
who joined just for the sport.

02:09.080 --> 02:10.039
[Tommy] Go on, go.

02:10.120 --> 02:11.800
I want men who've served in here.

02:11.920 --> 02:13.560
-All right, Pol.
-[Polly sobbing]

02:14.640 --> 02:16.120
[nurse] Mrs. Gray, please.

02:16.200 --> 02:18.800
-[Polly sobbing] Don't go, don't go...
-Pol. Pol.

02:18.880 --> 02:20.080
[doctor] He's in good hands.

02:20.160 --> 02:22.560
[Tommy] Polly? Pol? Pol?

02:22.920 --> 02:25.600
Go fuck those bastards responsible!

02:25.680 --> 02:27.120
All right, let them do their job,
all right?

02:27.200 --> 02:29.160
Let them do their job, I'll get soldiers.

02:29.240 --> 02:31.240
I'll be back. I'll be back.

02:39.880 --> 02:41.240
[door opens]

02:57.240 --> 02:58.440
[Tommy] Is it him?

03:00.480 --> 03:01.640
[Arthur] Yeah.

03:05.760 --> 03:07.000
On his own doorstep.

03:10.120 --> 03:12.320
His own fucking doorstep.

03:18.920 --> 03:21.760
[Tommy] Right, get out. And you, out.

03:30.040 --> 03:32.760
[Arthur screaming] What the fuck?

03:36.080 --> 03:37.360
[door closes behind]

03:41.480 --> 03:42.720
[Arthur] Poor John.

03:44.800 --> 03:46.360
Fucking gone.

03:47.960 --> 03:49.680
In the bleak midwinter.

03:52.240 --> 03:53.640
We promised, Arthur.

03:53.720 --> 03:55.120
[Arthur] I can't.

03:56.600 --> 03:58.200
In Hull we promised.

04:00.040 --> 04:03.600
-[Arthur] Can't look at him.
-Come here, brother.

04:07.560 --> 04:10.280
Come here. Come on, come here.

04:15.200 --> 04:16.880
[Arthur] Oh, my!

04:21.000 --> 04:22.400
Now say it.

04:26.560 --> 04:28.080
Say it, brother.

04:33.720 --> 04:35.960
In the bleak midwinter.

04:40.760 --> 04:41.920
[door opens]

04:42.040 --> 04:43.280
[Esme shouting] You're cursed.

04:43.360 --> 04:45.200
And I curse you again.
Look what have you done.

04:45.280 --> 04:47.400
What have you done?
Look at what you've done.

04:48.520 --> 04:50.720
Come here, come here.

04:52.600 --> 04:53.760
[Tommy sighs]

05:07.400 --> 05:09.080
Leave me with him.

05:09.600 --> 05:13.240
You've spoken to him, now it's my turn.

05:16.720 --> 05:17.880
Arthur?

05:22.120 --> 05:25.040
No peace for either of you, ever.

05:41.080 --> 05:42.720
Our stuff will stay with me.

05:48.800 --> 05:50.680
Taking the children on the road...

05:51.640 --> 05:53.600
to be with decent people.

05:57.520 --> 05:59.800
They'll never know
the cursed side of this family.

06:03.440 --> 06:05.040
We're done with them, John.

06:10.560 --> 06:12.000
[footsteps leaving]

06:22.560 --> 06:24.160
[Tommy sighs]

06:39.040 --> 06:41.840
-[knocking at door]
-[man] Tommy, they're gathered.

06:42.800 --> 06:44.160
[footsteps leaving]

07:17.040 --> 07:18.760
[footsteps approaching]

07:32.760 --> 07:33.960
John is dead.

07:37.200 --> 07:39.400
Esme's gone on the road with the Lees.

07:40.320 --> 07:41.720
She's taken the kids.

07:43.720 --> 07:47.640
Michael is badly wounded,
they say it's 60/40 in his favor.

07:47.720 --> 07:50.400
There's no number, there's no percentages.

07:52.360 --> 07:56.160
So the hand, the hand
beneath him stops him falling.

07:57.200 --> 07:58.760
Spoke to someone...

07:59.920 --> 08:01.160
my son will live.

08:03.680 --> 08:06.120
Michael and John were shot
because we killed someone...

08:08.160 --> 08:09.680
Vicente Changretta.

08:10.000 --> 08:12.160
[Tommy] His son Luca
has come to take revenge.

08:13.320 --> 08:16.040
Men from New York
and Sicily here in Birmingham.

08:16.760 --> 08:20.000
These men will not leave our city
until our whole family is dead.

08:20.560 --> 08:22.640
That's how it works, an eye for an eye.

08:24.000 --> 08:26.600
-It's called vendetta.
-[Arthur] Yeah, well...

08:28.960 --> 08:30.600
The bullet's been written...

08:32.400 --> 08:34.120
It says Luca.

08:35.720 --> 08:37.240
When the time comes...

08:38.960 --> 08:40.480
and it will come...

08:42.280 --> 08:44.080
Me as the oldest brother...

08:45.960 --> 08:49.840
will put this bullet
into his fucking head.

08:57.120 --> 08:58.760
There's been some bad blood between us.

08:59.000 --> 09:00.080
[laughs]

09:02.720 --> 09:03.800
[Polly laughs]

09:03.880 --> 09:05.200
[Arthur] Polly, please?

09:08.280 --> 09:11.680
Until this business is
settled we stay together.

09:12.600 --> 09:13.920
We stay here...

09:14.880 --> 09:18.360
Small Heath, Bordesley,
Hay Mills down to Greet.

09:18.480 --> 09:20.440
We know every face,

09:20.520 --> 09:22.360
every man is a soldier in this army.

09:24.000 --> 09:26.360
These men are professionals
and they're good at what they do,

09:26.480 --> 09:27.800
so we're gonna need more than we have.

09:29.400 --> 09:31.680
I sent a message to Aberama Gold.

09:32.280 --> 09:34.920
No. No, Tom.

09:35.400 --> 09:38.880
I'll get you 50 Lee boys. Good men, Tom.

09:38.960 --> 09:41.040
I don't need good men, Johnny,
for this, I need bad men.

09:41.120 --> 09:43.600
[Dogs] Tommy, his people
are fucking savages.

09:43.680 --> 09:45.560
You know, heathens, Tom.

09:45.640 --> 09:47.560
[Dogs] They don't
even let them in the fair,

09:47.640 --> 09:51.000
so they come and steal our horses.
You know, stealing from their own, Tom.

09:51.480 --> 09:53.920
So this is the plan, Thomas?

09:55.160 --> 09:56.680
This is the plan?

09:57.560 --> 10:01.960
Bullet with a name on it,
help from a bunch of savages.

10:04.080 --> 10:05.680
We're gonna go on the offensive.

10:06.160 --> 10:07.520
I've spoken to Moss.

10:07.640 --> 10:09.000
[Polly scoffs] Moss?

10:09.120 --> 10:10.600
I've spoken to Moss.

10:12.000 --> 10:17.120
Moss is putting out word, eyes,
and ears so we can find them.

10:19.680 --> 10:22.360
Yeah, the truth is that the police
are busy with the revolution.

10:23.080 --> 10:25.360
Moss says they're
expecting strikes and riots

10:25.480 --> 10:27.200
when the weather gets warm
and the Bolsheviks are planning to--

10:27.280 --> 10:29.400
The Bolsheviks
couldn't plan a fucking picnic.

10:29.480 --> 10:31.240
-He's reading the wrong papers.
-Ada!

10:32.480 --> 10:36.640
Real, or not real, the coppers
don't give a fuck about us, all right?

10:39.920 --> 10:41.520
Which means...

10:42.640 --> 10:44.440
that here today in this room...

10:45.760 --> 10:48.160
we have to agree to
end this war between us.

10:52.560 --> 10:53.680
Take a vote.

10:56.840 --> 10:58.080
Peace.

10:59.480 --> 11:01.720
I was never a part of this, but...

11:01.800 --> 11:03.040
peace.

11:03.680 --> 11:04.720
Peace.

11:06.160 --> 11:07.280
Peace.

11:08.600 --> 11:09.560
Peace.

11:10.080 --> 11:11.280
Shut up, Finn.

11:11.800 --> 11:13.000
Why can't I say peace?

11:13.360 --> 11:15.720
Arthur, let him have his say.

11:16.280 --> 11:18.720
Finn? Sit at the table.

11:23.480 --> 11:24.560
Little bastard...

11:27.040 --> 11:29.160
Sending his fucking kid, Sergeant Major...

11:30.440 --> 11:31.840
to do men's work.

11:35.240 --> 11:37.520
My son's not here to speak...

11:43.480 --> 11:45.960
So I'll speak on behalf of us both.

11:51.560 --> 11:52.800
Truce.

11:58.680 --> 12:00.000
Five for peace.

12:00.640 --> 12:02.520
Two for truce, on abstention.

12:03.240 --> 12:04.400
Let's get on with the war.

12:10.240 --> 12:11.880
[indistinct chatter]

12:16.080 --> 12:19.120
Whoa. Curly!

12:19.200 --> 12:20.600
Here, catch.

12:24.800 --> 12:28.720
Nice one. Right, come on
then, boys, unload, unload.

13:01.920 --> 13:03.160
Hats?

13:06.960 --> 13:08.320
Who's in Villa goalkeeper?

13:09.160 --> 13:11.440
Dan Shevlin played in goal for the Blues,

13:11.520 --> 13:13.080
and Tommy Jackson for the Villa.

13:14.320 --> 13:15.480
Fair enough.

13:23.680 --> 13:25.440
-Charlie?
-What is it, Curly?

13:25.680 --> 13:27.120
We've run out of bullets, Charlie.

13:28.600 --> 13:30.120
We need more ammunition.

13:30.720 --> 13:32.160
More ammunition.

13:32.560 --> 13:34.480
Johnny, more ammo.

13:35.440 --> 13:37.840
[In Romani]
More bullets to Charlie's yard.

13:47.360 --> 13:49.480
[Finn] We're like sitting ducks
here if the wops come.

13:49.920 --> 13:52.760
Yeah, well, Tommy said
we should do it in the open.

14:08.240 --> 14:10.120
This is how John wanted to go.

14:11.560 --> 14:12.760
On this smoke...

14:13.520 --> 14:14.840
and the truth is...

14:15.760 --> 14:19.200
we died together once before.

14:20.400 --> 14:21.480
Arthur,

14:22.320 --> 14:23.480
me,

14:24.880 --> 14:26.160
Danny WhizzBang,

14:27.440 --> 14:28.680
Freddie Thorne,

14:29.600 --> 14:30.760
Jeremiah,

14:31.520 --> 14:32.720
and John.

14:34.840 --> 14:36.520
We were cut off from the retreat...

14:37.800 --> 14:43.200
No bullets left, waiting for the Prussian
cavalry to come and to finish us off.

14:45.800 --> 14:47.360
And while we waited...

14:49.360 --> 14:53.200
Jeremiah said, "We should sing,
'In the Bleak Midwinter.'"

14:56.120 --> 14:57.440
But we were spared.

14:58.680 --> 15:00.000
The enemy never came.

15:00.760 --> 15:02.160
And we all agreed...

15:05.240 --> 15:08.400
That everything after that was extra.

15:09.840 --> 15:11.360
And when our time came...

15:12.280 --> 15:15.440
-we'd all remember.
-[Polly] You remember that God spared you.

15:17.080 --> 15:19.920
But what did you do with that
extra time that He gave you, eh, Thomas?

15:31.920 --> 15:33.560
[In Italian] Over here, this is the place.

15:34.000 --> 15:35.160
[In Italian] Keep watch.

15:35.600 --> 15:37.000
[In Italian] Let the smoke clear.

16:47.520 --> 16:48.760
[all clamoring]

16:49.640 --> 16:52.040
[Tommy] At ease. At ease.

16:52.160 --> 16:54.000
Do not return fire.

16:54.080 --> 16:58.640
I repeat, do not return fire. Stand down.

16:59.200 --> 17:02.040
The men doing the firing are on our side.

17:17.880 --> 17:18.720
[gunshot]

17:22.920 --> 17:24.240
[Tommy] I took the trouble...

17:25.000 --> 17:28.720
of getting an invitation to Aberama Gold.

17:29.640 --> 17:32.360
Oh, fuck! Now it's begun.

17:37.760 --> 17:39.960
Put us out in the open on purpose.

17:41.800 --> 17:44.560
To use John's funeral
fire as a fucking beacon.

17:45.480 --> 17:46.960
[Arthur] We were never
in any danger Polly.

17:47.040 --> 17:48.120
You set a trap.

17:48.400 --> 17:52.640
Finn? Finn? Go to
the yard and light the fires.

17:52.760 --> 17:55.520
You set a trap with us as fucking bait.

17:57.520 --> 17:58.920
Who's dead?

18:00.080 --> 18:01.440
[Tommy] Our enemies.

18:05.440 --> 18:08.520
-Who's dead?
-You wanna know, Pol?

18:09.040 --> 18:11.640
Two fucking no good Italians
who heard about the vendetta,

18:11.760 --> 18:14.200
trying to make a fucking name
for themselves, that's who.

18:14.360 --> 18:17.160
We got word to them about the funeral,

18:17.240 --> 18:18.520
the where, the when...

18:19.160 --> 18:21.120
Told them where to
stand for the best shot.

18:22.480 --> 18:24.840
[Tommy] And Aberama Gold
will do the rest.

18:25.720 --> 18:27.480
That's the language of vendetta,

18:27.560 --> 18:30.520
they take one of ours,
we take two of theirs.

18:31.800 --> 18:35.240
You used your own brother's funeral.

18:39.960 --> 18:42.400
When did we vote on this, Tommy?

18:45.080 --> 18:49.160
Curly, get a boat ready
to take the bodies to the city.

18:49.240 --> 18:51.760
And another boat for anyone,

18:51.840 --> 18:54.480
anyone who wants no more part of this.

18:54.600 --> 18:56.200
'Cause this is how it's gonna be.

18:57.640 --> 18:58.680
Polly!

19:00.960 --> 19:04.280
I'm not staying for this, Arthur,
I'm going home.

19:04.720 --> 19:07.960
Oh, yeah? Home's 57, Watery Lane.

19:08.680 --> 19:13.280
You go there, lock the doors,
you wait for me.

19:26.360 --> 19:27.400
Key to number 57.

19:27.480 --> 19:28.640
I'm going home to the country.

19:28.720 --> 19:31.520
You will stay here until it's over.
Take the fucking key, Linda.

19:32.000 --> 19:34.520
Look, away from here
you are a weakness to all of us.

19:34.600 --> 19:37.240
They will take you hostage and the baby,

19:37.320 --> 19:39.120
they will use the baby.

19:40.200 --> 19:45.120
Take it. Put the kettle on,
wait for your husband.

19:51.520 --> 19:53.800
The only way any of you
Shelbys will leave this place

19:53.920 --> 19:56.000
is in a cloud of smoke like John.

19:56.520 --> 19:58.760
Well, you're a Shelby now too, Linda.

20:03.320 --> 20:05.200
[Aberama] Five hundred each.
A thousand for the brace.

20:06.080 --> 20:07.480
Where do you want them?

20:07.560 --> 20:09.640
Charlie, take him to the yard.

20:43.200 --> 20:46.480
[Tommy] Not too far and not too deep,
Curly, I need the message to get through.

20:46.680 --> 20:48.000
Okay, Tommy.

20:48.480 --> 20:50.920
Finn, you go with him.

20:52.400 --> 20:54.640
Go on. Go with him.

20:56.440 --> 20:59.360
Oh, Tom, uh, tell Janet to
save me some goose, please?

21:18.640 --> 21:19.920
On your feet.

21:35.920 --> 21:37.320
Wait outside.

21:48.720 --> 21:50.120
[Michael] Hello, Mum.

21:54.280 --> 21:56.120
[Polly] They said you'd be out cold.

21:59.840 --> 22:01.880
-No, don't move.
-[Michael sighs]

22:02.680 --> 22:04.640
The doctor said they've
sewn you up like a football.

22:05.120 --> 22:06.400
What else did he say?

22:08.040 --> 22:09.520
He said you took four bullets.

22:10.840 --> 22:14.240
Only one was live, one was ricochet...

22:16.960 --> 22:18.320
Two were already spent.

22:21.760 --> 22:23.560
Two passed through John.

22:26.560 --> 22:28.680
Last thing I remember was his face.

22:33.800 --> 22:34.880
I watched him go.

22:41.880 --> 22:43.520
Where's Tommy?

22:43.920 --> 22:46.000
Oh, don't worry about Tommy,
just get better.

22:46.080 --> 22:47.160
Where is he?

22:49.480 --> 22:50.680
Tommy's back.

22:51.680 --> 22:55.840
We're all back. Garrison,
Watery Lane, Charlie's...

23:01.200 --> 23:03.360
-I need a cigarette.
-No.

23:07.000 --> 23:07.920
I made a decision.

23:08.000 --> 23:09.680
Give me a cigarette, Mum.

23:09.840 --> 23:12.400
-It's not allowed.
-It's not allowed?

23:24.480 --> 23:27.440
-I've decided we're gonna get away.
-Mmm, yeah?

23:28.480 --> 23:31.160
Just me and you, Australia.

23:33.000 --> 23:35.520
I've been to the Cunard office
and I've got a magazine.

23:36.080 --> 23:38.560
-[Michael] Hmm.
-Now, America's no good

23:38.640 --> 23:41.280
because that's where they are,
but there's no Italians in Australia.

23:41.360 --> 23:43.200
I think there are, Mum.

23:44.000 --> 23:45.320
Bad ones, I mean.

23:46.240 --> 23:47.880
Have you stopped taking
those prison tablets?

23:47.960 --> 23:49.360
Don't you worry about me.

23:49.440 --> 23:50.680
Well, I do.

23:51.120 --> 23:53.000
And just you get better.

23:53.760 --> 23:57.000
The doctor says it'll be five weeks,
then you'll be up and walking.

23:57.760 --> 24:01.040
Now, there's a boat that
leaves on February, the 13th.

24:01.120 --> 24:02.960
Tell Tommy I need to see him.

24:03.760 --> 24:06.600
Tell him to bring a gun small enough
to fit in the chamber pot, in case--

24:06.680 --> 24:08.200
You have to ban Tommy from coming.

24:11.640 --> 24:13.160
Also, the factory's coming
back to work tomorrow.

24:13.240 --> 24:14.640
Just forget about the fucking factories.

24:14.720 --> 24:16.280
Is there any word of
them going out on strike?

24:16.360 --> 24:17.600
We can go to Australia,

24:18.600 --> 24:20.960
it'll just be you, me, and your sister,

24:21.080 --> 24:24.080
'cause I've found out where she's buried,
somewhere outside Melbourne.

24:24.200 --> 24:25.440
Listen, Mum...

24:29.360 --> 24:31.360
I'm gonna get better slowly.

24:32.480 --> 24:34.280
But you need to get better fast.

24:36.040 --> 24:38.680
Without you he falls apart
and without him...

24:41.680 --> 24:43.480
without him they'll take us all.

24:45.280 --> 24:47.400
You've got to get us through this.

24:50.320 --> 24:51.840
And I promise...

24:52.680 --> 24:55.360
I'll board that train with you
and we'll go to Australia.

25:30.680 --> 25:34.680
I like your yard, Mr. Strong.
How much would you take for it?

25:36.960 --> 25:38.400
It's not for sale.

25:38.800 --> 25:40.080
Not for sale?

25:40.840 --> 25:42.120
Okay.

25:54.200 --> 25:58.240
I just took a look around.
I like this place.

26:00.040 --> 26:03.440
Fire for melting silver,
canal to get it away.

26:04.520 --> 26:05.640
[Aberama] How much?

26:09.560 --> 26:11.680
Nothing you see here
is for sale, Mr. Gold.

26:11.800 --> 26:14.360
Oh, everything's for sale, everything.

26:19.880 --> 26:23.520
You tell Mr. Strong
I'm going to buy his yard.

26:25.200 --> 26:27.440
This yard has been in
his family since they settled.

26:27.560 --> 26:29.920
But I've decided to
make it part of our deal.

26:36.600 --> 26:37.880
Charlie?

26:40.400 --> 26:41.720
Charlie, come here.

26:46.920 --> 26:48.800
I'm gonna spin a coin
for your yard, Charlie.

26:49.960 --> 26:51.160
You're going to what?

26:51.680 --> 26:53.040
If it's heads...

26:53.840 --> 26:57.080
Abbie here takes all
of this with my blessing.

26:57.640 --> 26:58.760
Tommy?

27:00.000 --> 27:01.600
And if it's tails...

27:04.120 --> 27:06.400
I fuck your daughter, Mr. Gold.

27:09.320 --> 27:10.680
[Arthur laughing]

27:10.760 --> 27:12.680
You have three daughters I hear,

27:13.040 --> 27:16.640
and Esmeralda is the oldest
and also the prettiest,

27:16.760 --> 27:18.440
so I'll have her.

27:18.520 --> 27:21.400
So make her part of the deal
and spin against the yard.

27:22.120 --> 27:23.960
Tommy, for fuck's sake.

27:24.400 --> 27:27.800
Here, you toss the coin, Mr. Gold.

27:41.760 --> 27:42.960
No.

27:44.960 --> 27:47.120
Please don't believe this is a joke,

27:47.240 --> 27:50.120
the coin toss is sacred, yes, Arthur?

27:50.200 --> 27:51.480
Sacred.

27:55.320 --> 27:58.520
You toss that coin, you take
a bet before witnesses,

27:58.920 --> 28:00.280
and if I win...

28:01.320 --> 28:02.720
Then we'll insist

28:03.480 --> 28:09.720
that the terms of this
agreement wager are fulfilled.

28:14.240 --> 28:15.680
Toss the coin, Mr. Gold.

28:33.040 --> 28:36.320
Tommy Shelby, OBE...

28:40.640 --> 28:42.320
No wager today...

28:43.320 --> 28:46.680
But with this penny I will buy
a flower to put on your grave...

28:49.480 --> 28:50.960
When the time comes.

28:53.640 --> 28:55.120
And before that time...

28:56.440 --> 29:01.520
Please don't again disrespect
my friends or their valued property.

29:03.680 --> 29:06.280
We missed Christmas, let's have it now.

29:08.000 --> 29:10.240
Peace on earth, goodwill to all men.

29:13.680 --> 29:14.960
Drink?

29:20.680 --> 29:21.840
Oh, my God.

29:22.600 --> 29:25.560
Toast to my brother, John,
raise your glasses all of you.

29:25.960 --> 29:28.920
Raise them up, all of you.

29:29.600 --> 29:30.840
John Shelby.

29:30.920 --> 29:31.880
[all] Cheers!

29:31.960 --> 29:35.200
I've got wire, I've got chains,
I've got the steel rods. Now what?

29:35.320 --> 29:37.840
Right, put the steel rods
up the geese's arse,

29:37.960 --> 29:40.160
and then you put those
steel rods above the fire,

29:40.240 --> 29:42.440
spinning the rod to stop
the bird from burning.

29:43.000 --> 29:44.560
[all] Cheers!

29:45.240 --> 29:46.200
[Dogs] You did get potatoes, Charlie?

29:46.280 --> 29:48.480
No, I didn't get fucking potatoes,
what's the matter with you?

29:48.640 --> 29:51.600
I don't want that bastard Aberama Gold

29:51.680 --> 29:54.000
putting it around that we live
like diddy guys.

29:54.120 --> 29:58.680
I want him to see that we live proper,
that we eat proper, in the proper way.

29:58.760 --> 30:00.640
What the fuck are you talking about?

30:00.720 --> 30:02.400
I'll get the potatoes meself.

30:09.840 --> 30:12.400
Never thought my high
fucking heels from Paris

30:12.480 --> 30:16.320
would be stepping through
the horse shit of Small Heath ever again.

30:16.440 --> 30:18.080
Temporary, Polly.

30:18.160 --> 30:20.480
[Lizzie] Yeah, until hell freezes over.

31:00.240 --> 31:01.840
[Tommy clears throat] How's Michael?

31:02.400 --> 31:06.160
You said eight men from New York,
seven from Sicily, at £500 each?

31:07.120 --> 31:08.720
Course you can afford it.

31:09.200 --> 31:10.680
Most we'll do ourselves.

31:11.880 --> 31:14.240
How many of your factories
are in the safe territories?

31:14.320 --> 31:15.520
Seventy percent.

31:16.440 --> 31:19.120
Shelby Company Limited
will continue to manufacture.

31:21.560 --> 31:23.880
Lizzie says its only whores these days?

31:25.080 --> 31:26.200
Yeah.

31:28.600 --> 31:30.600
Have them smuggled in, I suppose.

31:34.120 --> 31:35.480
I need you back, Polly.

31:35.600 --> 31:37.640
Paying whores for sex.

31:37.720 --> 31:39.320
Paying killers to kill.

31:39.400 --> 31:40.600
Yep, nothing for nothing.

31:40.720 --> 31:42.040
Poor Thomas.

31:45.480 --> 31:46.960
You know, our mother went this way,

31:47.080 --> 31:51.280
spirits and ghosts, tablets,
fucking, fucking séances,

31:51.360 --> 31:53.320
scaring us kids halfway to fuckin' death.

31:53.400 --> 31:56.360
Yeah, well, I'm not going that way...

31:58.240 --> 32:00.520
'Cause in the smoke I realized something.

32:03.520 --> 32:05.040
I'm just like you now...

32:05.920 --> 32:07.440
You and Arthur.

32:09.720 --> 32:13.600
I was dead in that noose
and then I was saved,

32:13.720 --> 32:15.680
so everything from now on is extra.

32:16.200 --> 32:18.560
What I didn't understand until today

32:19.480 --> 32:21.080
is when you're dead already...

32:23.680 --> 32:25.080
You're free.

32:29.320 --> 32:30.960
Fucking love it.

32:34.200 --> 32:36.720
I'll help you 'cause
Michael wants me to help you.

32:38.880 --> 32:40.360
Starting now...

32:41.440 --> 32:45.800
Get your whores vetted by Lizzie,
they'll use your weakness.

32:45.920 --> 32:49.720
Don't take any new men on in the factories
unless you know their families.

32:52.520 --> 32:57.400
And your new friend, Mr. Aberama Gold...

32:58.480 --> 33:01.280
He wants something other than just money.

33:01.920 --> 33:05.760
I see things in the air around people,
but it's not just the tablets.

33:08.280 --> 33:09.640
It's real.

33:10.720 --> 33:13.080
Ask him what he really wants.

33:52.960 --> 33:54.520
[Tommy] She's a fine beast this one.

33:57.600 --> 34:00.120
Nothing you see is for sale, Mr. Shelby.

34:05.640 --> 34:08.239
You know your grandfather
camped with mine for a while?

34:09.000 --> 34:10.239
Yep...

34:10.840 --> 34:11.960
And it didn't last.

34:12.040 --> 34:16.199
No, it ended badly
in a pub called the Wenlock.

34:17.239 --> 34:19.840
We were owed money, still are.

34:27.880 --> 34:32.480
Other than money, and this
scrapyard that's clearly not for sale,

34:34.480 --> 34:36.480
what is it that you
want from me, Mr. Gold?

34:38.239 --> 34:40.159
How do you know I want anything from you?

34:41.600 --> 34:45.480
Well, because my company treasurer

34:45.560 --> 34:49.239
is a certified accountant
and also apparently, a witch.

34:51.760 --> 34:53.440
You're a sporting man.

34:55.679 --> 34:58.120
-Yes, I am.
-And a gambling man.

35:01.840 --> 35:04.360
Bonnie, take off your shirt.

35:08.560 --> 35:13.000
What I want from you, Mr. Shelby,
is for you to take a gamble on him.

35:13.320 --> 35:16.320
I want you to help
my son achieve his ambition.

35:19.640 --> 35:21.760
[grunting]

35:23.840 --> 35:25.600
What ambition might that be?

35:57.520 --> 35:59.040
[knocking at door]

36:06.600 --> 36:08.360
[knocking at door]

36:10.640 --> 36:11.720
Who is it?

36:12.040 --> 36:13.240
[Moss] Inspector Moss.

36:22.000 --> 36:24.560
-I'm looking for Tommy.
-He's at Charlie's.

36:25.200 --> 36:27.960
But I wouldn't go there,
some bad men have arrived.

36:28.600 --> 36:29.800
Yes, I've heard.

36:29.920 --> 36:33.040
They'll be drunk by now, or worse.

36:34.040 --> 36:35.560
Can you take a message?

36:36.640 --> 36:41.240
The, uh, intelligence service in London
have sent seven officers up to Birmingham

36:41.400 --> 36:44.920
to take over the investigation
into the Communists and seditionists.

36:45.040 --> 36:48.120
They've got a list of people
of great interest and, uh,

36:48.200 --> 36:51.720
top of that list is
Ada Thorne, formerly Shelby.

36:51.800 --> 36:55.280
Now, they seem to think that
she came back from New York

36:55.360 --> 36:57.920
to organize a revolution in Birmingham.

36:58.080 --> 36:59.560
Well, tell them they're wrong.

37:00.080 --> 37:02.840
Ada Shelby's now firmly back
in place in the family business.

37:02.920 --> 37:05.840
Thing is, when you get
a mark against your name...

37:06.480 --> 37:08.680
It's very hard to shift it.

37:09.560 --> 37:11.000
They will come looking for her.

37:11.080 --> 37:14.720
Well, tell them there's a queue,
everybody wants a Shelby.

37:15.960 --> 37:17.400
Mrs. Shelby...

37:18.560 --> 37:23.640
These are military men,
no less dangerous than any Italians.

37:24.240 --> 37:27.760
You tell Tommy, I think he'll understand.

37:29.760 --> 37:31.840
Say good night to you then.

38:07.400 --> 38:10.000
-Hello, Curly.
-Oh, hello.

38:10.080 --> 38:13.520
I'm here to collect Mr. Shelby's
horse, I'm a little early I think.

38:13.640 --> 38:16.640
Are you gonna train the horse to race?

38:16.760 --> 38:17.840
Yes.

38:18.320 --> 38:21.000
Don't use a crop, she's very gentle.

38:21.400 --> 38:23.120
-I won't.
-[Charlie] Yo.

38:23.200 --> 38:24.440
I, I...

38:28.120 --> 38:29.960
[May] How is Mr. Shelby?

38:30.440 --> 38:33.920
[Curly] Well, now he's got the Americans
after him, he's a bit hard on people.

38:34.240 --> 38:36.880
-The Americans?
-Yeah, they call them the mafia.

38:37.960 --> 38:39.040
Dear God.

38:39.160 --> 38:42.280
Yeah, there's 15 of them,
yeah, they want to kill us all,

38:42.360 --> 38:46.280
but we got guns and grenades and, and
Polly's back, so it's gonna be okay, yeah.

38:47.400 --> 38:48.560
I'll get the horse.

39:09.760 --> 39:12.160
Mr. Shelby, thank God
you're back, we've got real trouble.

39:12.680 --> 39:14.000
[Tommy] What meetings do I have today?

39:14.080 --> 39:17.560
Um, well, uh, there's a supplier
from Coventry at 10:00,

39:17.640 --> 39:19.920
uh, the convener with
the boiler makers unit at 11:00,

39:20.040 --> 39:21.520
and then Chamber of Commerce at 1:00.

39:21.640 --> 39:22.720
But that isn't the thing...

39:22.800 --> 39:25.120
Right. Clear the space here,
move this car back,

39:25.240 --> 39:26.920
give me 100 feet of rope and a bell.

39:27.480 --> 39:29.320
-Rope?
-Yep, and a bell.

39:29.880 --> 39:31.920
Mr. Shelby, this place
is about to explode.

39:32.080 --> 39:33.680
A rope and a bell, Devlin.

39:33.800 --> 39:35.160
Bonnie, this way.

39:35.320 --> 39:36.600
Yes, Mr. Shelby.

39:37.720 --> 39:41.360
And by the way, the revolution
is scheduled to begin at noon.

39:43.600 --> 39:46.240
[Tommy] There he is, Billy Mills,

39:46.320 --> 39:48.320
former heavyweight champion,

39:48.400 --> 39:50.360
Staffordshire, Warwickshire,
and Worcestershire.

39:50.480 --> 39:51.520
Hello, Billy.

39:51.840 --> 39:52.720
Yeah.

39:53.200 --> 39:55.600
And now I work for you, Mr. Shelby,

39:55.720 --> 39:57.320
for not enough money.

39:58.720 --> 40:01.720
Right. That enough for you?

40:03.320 --> 40:04.520
Enough for what?

40:04.600 --> 40:08.280
Someone here who wants to fight you.
Bonnie Gold, come here, son.

40:09.800 --> 40:11.400
I'm a heavyweight,

40:11.520 --> 40:13.040
he's a welterweight at best.

40:13.160 --> 40:15.040
Nevertheless, he wants to fight you.

40:16.800 --> 40:20.600
Yeah. And when I damage him,
the Blinders will take my eyes.

40:21.240 --> 40:24.640
No comeback, Billy,
just a fight, Queensberry rules.

40:25.640 --> 40:27.440
-[Billy] When?
-[Tommy] Now.

40:27.520 --> 40:28.840
-[Billy] Where?
-[Tommy] Here.

40:28.920 --> 40:32.800
[Arthur] All right, you lot, come here,
make it interesting

40:32.880 --> 40:35.160
and place a wager with me,

40:35.240 --> 40:36.880
I'll give you good odds.

40:36.960 --> 40:40.600
I'll give you good odds on the boy,
even better on the big lad.

40:40.800 --> 40:42.720
Now, there you go, what you want?

40:45.560 --> 40:47.200
[Aberama] Bonnie, come here, son.

40:54.200 --> 40:55.840
[indistinct chatter]

41:02.240 --> 41:03.320
[rock musing playing]

41:03.400 --> 41:06.520
♪ Voices in my head ♪

41:06.640 --> 41:08.640
♪ You know what they said ♪

41:09.040 --> 41:12.200
♪ Stand your ground
Never walk away ♪

41:12.280 --> 41:13.600
♪ They said ♪

41:13.680 --> 41:17.680
♪ Every cell of my DNA ♪

41:17.800 --> 41:22.480
♪ I don't have to go down on my knees ♪

41:23.160 --> 41:25.680
♪ It's in my blood ♪ 

41:25.800 --> 41:28.080
♪ It's in my blood ♪ 

41:28.160 --> 41:30.680
♪ It's in my blood ♪ 

41:30.760 --> 41:33.280
Your boy knows he can hit back, right?

41:34.000 --> 41:36.520
Told him in the professional game
people want their money's worth,

41:36.600 --> 41:38.080
don't win too fast.

41:42.360 --> 41:43.840
But if you've seen enough...

41:46.560 --> 41:48.200
Finish him, Bonnie.

41:54.720 --> 41:57.640
Fuck me. That was a punch.

41:58.320 --> 42:00.320
What's he got, horseshoes
in those gloves or what?

42:00.480 --> 42:03.920
No, just his dad's strength
and his mother's temper.

42:09.000 --> 42:11.400
-[Tommy] Does he have fits?
-[Aberama] No.

42:11.520 --> 42:13.160
-Asthma?
-No.

42:13.240 --> 42:14.440
How's he cut?

42:14.520 --> 42:16.720
Well, no one's cut him
yet, but his skin's thick.

42:17.000 --> 42:19.920
-Does he drink?
-Water, sometimes.

42:20.000 --> 42:23.200
-How many fights?
-Twenty-five, bare knuckle, all knockouts,

42:23.280 --> 42:25.600
five with gloves
in pastures, all knockouts.

42:25.720 --> 42:27.640
Against Romany fighters?

42:27.720 --> 42:29.720
That's why they won't let us in the fairs
no more, he keeps winning.

42:29.800 --> 42:32.240
I could fight a fucking tree
and knock it out, Mr. Shelby.

42:32.320 --> 42:34.640
[Arthur laughs] I like him. I like him.

42:34.760 --> 42:36.520
Mrs. Eden is waiting upstairs.

42:36.600 --> 42:38.320
All right,
I'll be there in a minute. Arthur?

42:54.000 --> 42:55.480
Mr. Shelby's on his way.

42:55.560 --> 42:57.040
Fight in the workshop?

42:57.240 --> 43:00.000
No, no, just a bit of sport.

43:00.080 --> 43:01.080
Do you want some more tea?

43:01.200 --> 43:02.640
No, what kind of sport?

43:04.400 --> 43:06.800
Sport between men, Mrs. Eden.

43:08.200 --> 43:09.800
Actually, I'm Miss Eden.

43:11.680 --> 43:14.360
Be patient, a good man will come.

43:15.360 --> 43:18.280
-[Tommy] What do you think?
-[Arthur] No, that was over fast.

43:18.360 --> 43:20.080
Yeah, but you can tell, eh?

43:20.160 --> 43:22.640
I'd give him to the Irish boys
at Digbeth, they can teach him.

43:22.800 --> 43:25.480
-And then what?
-We promote him.

43:25.840 --> 43:28.640
Big money in London,
New York, if he's good.

43:28.760 --> 43:30.200
We control him.

43:30.280 --> 43:33.120
On the book, control
the odds, like a horse.

43:33.240 --> 43:37.360
Control when he wins, when he loses.
Alfie Solomons runs a fighter,

43:37.440 --> 43:41.120
says he's looking money for nothing,
fighting's been good for us,

43:41.200 --> 43:42.720
better if you know who's gonna win.

43:44.400 --> 43:46.760
But we got a lot going on, though, Tom.

43:46.880 --> 43:50.200
Yeah. That's why it'd be
good to have the kid around.

43:51.200 --> 43:52.680
We take the fucking kid on,

43:53.400 --> 43:55.960
one day he may end up
fucking taking a bullet for you.

43:56.440 --> 43:58.760
Can't afford to lose
another brother, can I?

43:58.840 --> 44:00.120
Hmm.

44:00.480 --> 44:03.760
No horses like him,
so he's all right.

44:04.440 --> 44:05.640
Come on.

44:09.720 --> 44:11.480
All right, we'll do it.

44:11.960 --> 44:14.360
[Aberama] But we need to
do a deal about money.

44:14.440 --> 44:16.880
-[Arthur] Mmm-hmm.
-Yeah. Speak to my accountant.

44:17.000 --> 44:18.360
-The witch?
-Yeah.

44:18.440 --> 44:20.920
So when will my first real
fight be do you think, Mr. Shelby?

44:21.040 --> 44:24.520
Ah, soon as we can find anyone
stupid enough to get in the ring with you.

44:25.440 --> 44:26.680
God help him.

44:27.760 --> 44:29.200
Here you go, Bonnie.

44:32.000 --> 44:33.720
You're a Peaky Blinder now, son.

44:42.800 --> 44:47.600
-There you go, the lion's share.
-Thank you.

45:00.400 --> 45:01.640
Mr. Shelby...

45:02.760 --> 45:04.520
I understand you've had a bereavement.

45:06.320 --> 45:08.320
[Tommy] I'm here to talk business.

45:09.520 --> 45:10.920
Then I'll be blunt.

45:11.880 --> 45:12.880
As will I.

45:13.000 --> 45:14.920
What you're doing here is robbery.

45:15.040 --> 45:16.320
Every man is free to leave.

45:16.400 --> 45:19.000
You cut five shillings
from the weekly wage.

45:19.120 --> 45:20.440
Mmm-hmm.

45:22.080 --> 45:24.920
And you said, if I did that

45:25.040 --> 45:28.320
you'd bring every man and woman
in all my factories out on strike.

45:29.000 --> 45:30.200
Yes.

45:32.680 --> 45:34.200
Do you have a whistle?

45:36.160 --> 45:38.520
If so, blow it.

45:44.080 --> 45:46.480
I thought at least we
might have a discussion,

45:47.360 --> 45:49.080
as I do with other owners.

45:49.760 --> 45:51.440
I'm not other owners.

45:52.000 --> 45:53.040
No.

45:54.520 --> 45:58.000
You believe because coal is expensive
and it's been a cold winter,

45:58.080 --> 46:00.280
and because wages have been
cut over the past twelve months,

46:00.400 --> 46:05.080
you think because of all that
nobody will walk out on strike,

46:06.160 --> 46:07.720
'cause no one can afford to.

46:09.360 --> 46:11.560
And until recently
that would've been true.

46:13.040 --> 46:15.120
But something has changed this past year.

46:16.600 --> 46:21.080
Cold winter in the east,
people are not afraid anymore.

46:21.200 --> 46:22.520
[scoffs]

46:24.600 --> 46:26.560
I have it on very good authority

46:26.640 --> 46:30.400
that the Bolsheviks couldn't
organize a fucking picnic.

46:33.000 --> 46:34.720
You know, Mr. Shelby...

46:36.240 --> 46:38.440
It's almost as if you want trouble.

46:45.640 --> 46:48.120
If the rule of law
in Birmingham were to break down

46:48.200 --> 46:52.760
during this period
of turmoil for my family,

46:52.840 --> 46:56.560
then yes, that would
indeed have its benefits

46:57.400 --> 46:59.640
for reasons you could not understand.

47:00.440 --> 47:01.800
I've heard rumors.

47:02.920 --> 47:04.600
Sport between men.

47:04.920 --> 47:07.560
Just blow your fucking whistle, eh?

47:17.920 --> 47:21.320
[door opens and closes]

47:26.480 --> 47:28.000
[whistle blowing]

47:30.720 --> 47:33.240
[man] Tools down for the clearing.

47:34.080 --> 47:36.880
All right. Come one, you.
Put your tools down.

47:49.640 --> 47:50.880
[man] Everyone out!

47:53.600 --> 47:55.280
I said, everyone out.

47:56.840 --> 47:58.440
Stop working immediately.

48:13.480 --> 48:14.520
[Tommy] Yeah, come in.

48:23.440 --> 48:24.880
I did say this would happen.

48:27.160 --> 48:28.360
Who's next?

48:28.760 --> 48:34.680
Um, he's a, a delegate from
the European Council for Trade.

48:35.800 --> 48:38.720
He's here to talk about
the import of car parts to France.

48:38.840 --> 48:41.280
-Right.
-He's come all the way from Paris,

48:42.160 --> 48:45.520
but given the circumstances
I could send him away?

48:48.880 --> 48:51.080
Given what circumstances? Send him in.

49:10.720 --> 49:16.360
Mr. Shelby, this is
Monsieur Paz, from Paris.

49:23.040 --> 49:24.600
I heard you had trouble.

49:26.800 --> 49:28.120
It's good of you to see me.

49:30.440 --> 49:32.360
You just came from Paris, eh?

49:37.880 --> 49:39.360
You know Paris?

49:40.880 --> 49:42.840
I left Paris in a cattle truck.

49:47.440 --> 49:48.920
And they said you were French.

49:51.160 --> 49:53.800
Well, I came here from Paris...

49:55.120 --> 49:57.040
That does not mean I'm French.

50:03.360 --> 50:04.920
Guess where I'm from?

50:10.080 --> 50:12.920
Well, in my cattle truck in Paris...

50:15.320 --> 50:20.560
There were American
soldiers who played cards.

50:26.520 --> 50:28.040
They sound like you.

50:28.720 --> 50:31.960
Yeah. Did you win?

50:34.360 --> 50:37.920
You didn't come on a train. Your
suit is pressed, your shoes are clean.

50:38.840 --> 50:40.200
Where do you get your suits made?

50:41.960 --> 50:45.720
I have a tailor in New York City.

50:47.560 --> 50:48.680
Look.

50:52.160 --> 50:55.440
Funacci, Italian.

50:56.480 --> 50:57.760
He's my uncle.

50:58.200 --> 51:00.600
He makes suits in
the basement of Mott Street.

51:02.320 --> 51:03.760
He is my uncle,

51:04.880 --> 51:07.800
so every stitch, stitched with blood.

51:10.800 --> 51:13.040
I heard you dress well, Mr. Shelby,

51:16.120 --> 51:17.440
but now I see...

51:19.000 --> 51:20.720
not so well as me.

51:27.800 --> 51:29.720
You know, I have uncles as well...

51:33.560 --> 51:38.280
But they're not the sort of men who would,
who would work in a basement

51:39.080 --> 51:40.720
with a needle and thread,

51:41.880 --> 51:43.480
Mr. Changretta.

51:50.800 --> 51:54.760
I am surprised how easy it was
to get into a room with you.

51:57.400 --> 51:58.720
And now?

52:02.160 --> 52:03.440
And now?

52:07.440 --> 52:09.440
And now you should know

52:10.400 --> 52:14.560
that during the trouble you
had earlier on your factory floor,

52:14.680 --> 52:18.920
I sent an accomplice
into your office in overalls.

52:22.160 --> 52:23.560
He found your gun...

52:26.440 --> 52:27.880
And unloaded it.

52:35.240 --> 52:36.640
Arthur Shelby,

52:39.200 --> 52:40.080
Polly Gray,

52:44.000 --> 52:45.400
Michael Gray,

52:48.560 --> 52:50.000
John Shelby,

52:58.040 --> 52:59.400
spent.

53:01.200 --> 53:03.120
Ada Thorne.

53:03.600 --> 53:05.520
And finally...

53:12.000 --> 53:13.560
Tommy Shelby.

53:19.200 --> 53:21.080
None of you will survive.

53:24.320 --> 53:26.960
Your level of security is pitiful

53:29.360 --> 53:34.320
and we are an organization
of a different dimension.

53:37.760 --> 53:40.600
I could've killed you when
I walked through the door...

53:42.000 --> 53:43.360
But you see...

53:45.760 --> 53:47.600
I want you to be the last.

53:50.560 --> 53:54.880
I want you to be alive after
your entire family is dead

53:55.720 --> 53:58.080
'cause my mother says

53:58.960 --> 54:01.440
that is what will hurt you the most.

54:04.800 --> 54:09.480
You people have
traditions of honor, as do we.

54:14.720 --> 54:18.640
Instead of sending you a black hand,
I could've had you killed in the night.

54:19.320 --> 54:21.080
You don't know why...

54:27.720 --> 54:30.200
But I want you to know why,

54:32.720 --> 54:39.480
and I wanna suggest to you that
we fight this vendetta with honor.

54:50.560 --> 54:53.560
No civilians, no children.

54:55.520 --> 54:56.560
No police.

54:59.360 --> 55:02.360
Welcome to Birmingham, Mr. Changretta.

55:04.320 --> 55:05.600
Grazie.

55:18.760 --> 55:22.960
Ah, that smell. I miss it.

55:23.080 --> 55:24.640
What, the shithouse?

55:26.120 --> 55:30.160
No. I don't know what it is.

55:31.600 --> 55:34.960
Birmingham, I suppose. Small Heath.

55:36.440 --> 55:39.760
-That smell.
-The smell brings it back.

55:41.240 --> 55:42.800
Where's John do you think, Tom?

55:44.320 --> 55:45.800
Fuck knows.

55:46.320 --> 55:48.080
Well, he's gone, though.

55:48.640 --> 55:50.360
Like a hole behind your head.

55:52.720 --> 55:54.800
Thought you thought there was a heaven.

55:55.320 --> 55:59.080
No. More like hell for our John.

56:00.520 --> 56:02.000
No, neither one.

56:04.240 --> 56:06.000
He's just not here anymore.

56:27.280 --> 56:30.080
It's like with Grace, Arthur...

56:31.120 --> 56:32.520
They're just gone,

56:33.080 --> 56:34.800
just fucking gone.

56:48.720 --> 56:51.280
["Red Right Hand" playing]

57:04.400 --> 57:06.800
♪ On a gathering storm comes ♪

57:06.880 --> 57:08.960
♪ A tall handsome man ♪

57:09.080 --> 57:10.840
♪ In a dusty black coat with ♪

57:10.920 --> 57:13.160
♪ A red right hand ♪
